#!/bin/sh
# check-engine-claiming-specs-use-probe.shs — ratchet guard against the
# "spec claims to guard a compiled lane but physically runs interpreted" bug class.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# A `*_spec.spl` executed by `bin/simple test` CANNOT run under the Cranelift JIT
# or the native lane. Four stacked layers force the tree-walking interpreter, and
# none of them is opt-out-able from inside a spec file (line numbers verified
# 2026-08-09 against src/compiler_rust/driver/src/exec_core.rs):
#
#   L1  The runner pins the child engine before spawning it:
#       src/app/test_runner_new/test_runner_single.spl:811
#       src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/test_runner/test_runner_execute.spl:86
#       src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/test_runner/test_runner_single.spl:170
#         -> env_set("SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE", "interpret")
#   L2  The driver honours the pin: exec_core.rs:120 maps
#       "interpret"|"interpreter" -> ExecutionMode::Interpret; the JIT arm is
#       gated behind `self.execution_mode.is_jit()` (exec_core.rs:933/936).
#   L3  Even with the JIT forced ON, a module with no `fn main` de-JITs to
#       `evaluate_module` (exec_core.rs:696-703 and :1074-1079). A spec file is
#       top-level describe/it with no main, so L3 alone suffices.
#   L4  The obvious workaround -- "give the spec a `fn main` so L3 stops firing"
#       -- IS ALREADY DELIBERATELY CLOSED (exec_core.rs:1085-1100, added for
#       doc/08_tracking/bug/bare_assert_statement_vacuity_2026-08-02.md OPEN 3).
#       With a `main` present the JIT entry path calls only `main`, so every
#       module-level describe/it is DROPPED: zero examples, exit 0, a
#       deliberately-failing example invisible. The driver therefore bails out to
#       the interpreter for any file with module-level examples. So adding `main`
#       cannot lift L3 -- it either de-JITs anyway, or (examples nested inside
#       main) makes the file vacuous instead.
#
# And there is nothing to demote FROM: `describe`/`it`/`expect` are Rust
# INTERPRETER INTRINSICS with thread-local state
# (src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_call/bdd.rs). There is no codegen
# lowering for them. A spec body physically cannot execute under a compiled lane.
#
# CONSEQUENCE: any spec whose titles claim to guard JIT / native / codegen / AOT
# behaviour is asserting against the interpreter, and stays GREEN through a total
# reintroduction of the defect. Proven 2026-08-01: with the real codegen arm
# sabotaged, 11/11 in-process examples PASSED while the out-of-process arm failed
# with the exact original 4-wrong count.
#
# THE SUPPORTED MECHANISM
#   src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/spec/engine_probe.spl — the spec stays on the
#   interpreter and spawns a runnable probe PROGRAM (one WITH `fn main`) under a
#   named engine, scoring its verdict LINE. Guide:
#   doc/07_guide/infra/testing/spec_engine_reach.md
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
#   Flags a spec as ENGINE-CLAIMING when a `describe`/`it` TITLE mentions a
#   compiled lane (jit, cranelift, native codegen, natively, aot). Such a spec
#   must do ONE of:
#     (a) use the engine_probe API (run_under_engine / engine_stdout /
#         engine_verdict_passes / engine_probe), or
#     (b) carry an explicit acknowledgement marker:
#           # @engine-reach: interpreter-only — <reason>
#         which records that the author KNOWS the claim is interpreter-scoped, or
#     (c) carry the compiled-lane marker:
#           # @engine-reach: native-by-compile — <reason>
#         for a spec that reaches a compiled lane through a DIFFERENT concrete
#         mechanism than engine_probe: it shells out to a real
#         `compile --native` / `native-build`, then RUNS the produced binary and
#         scores its output. That is strictly stronger evidence than the probe
#         API, so calling such a spec "interpreter-only" would be a lie. The
#         marker is NOT taken on faith: the body must also show both halves of
#         the pattern (a native-compile invocation AND a process-execution call),
#         the same shape-level check applied to engine_probe usage.
#
#   It is a RATCHET, not a retrofit: 228 pre-existing offenders are listed in the
#   baseline file and are NOT failures. Only a spec that is engine-claiming,
#   unprobed, unacknowledged AND not in the baseline fails. Existing specs are
#   untouched; nothing currently green can turn red.
#
#   Removing a file from the baseline is the intended way to retire debt.
#   Adding one requires editing the baseline in the same commit, which is the
#   review checkpoint this guard exists to create.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: scanning 0 files is an ERROR (exit 2), never a PASS. --selftest
# runs before every scan and is fatal.
#
# TWO INPUT MODES — and only ONE of them is valid for gating a PUSH
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
#   --root DIR   scans the FILESYSTEM working tree. Correct for interactive use.
#   --ref <rev>  scans the COMMITTED content at <rev>: paths from
#                `git ls-tree -r --name-only <rev>`, bodies from
#                `git cat-file blob <rev>:<path>`, and the BASELINE itself read
#                from <rev> too.
#
# Why --ref exists (2026-08-17): this guard is invoked by
# scripts/check/pre-push-conflict-tree-guard.shs to gate a PUSH, but a push only
# ever sends COMMITTED content. Scanning the working tree made it fail on files
# that cannot possibly be in any push. Measured on this host: of 56 reported
# offenders, 52 were already committed at origin/main (other lanes' pre-existing
# debt) and **4 were UNTRACKED scratch files** belonging to one of ~16 concurrent
# agent sessions sharing this single working copy — so one lane's temporary file
# blocked every other lane's push. This is the same failure, and the same fix,
# that check-test-tree-divergence.shs already took (see .claude/rules/vcs.md: it
# "reads COMMITTED content via git ls-tree/cat-file, never the shared working
# copy"). BOTH modes feed ONE shared analyzer (scan_one / is_engine_claiming /
# is_exempt, all of which take a BODY FILE, never a repo path) precisely so the
# two modes cannot drift apart; fixture 19 asserts they agree byte-for-byte on an
# identical committed tree.
#
# For the range-scoped escape used at push time (pre-existing debt must not block
# a landing that introduces nothing), see the companion
# scripts/check/check-engine-claiming-specs-use-probe-delta.shs.
#
# Usage:
#   sh scripts/check/check-engine-claiming-specs-use-probe.shs [--selftest]
#        [--root DIR | --ref REV] [--list] [--update-baseline] [--baseline FILE]
# Verdict is always the LAST line of stdout:
#   PASS  — <n> spec files scanned, <m> engine-claiming, <k> baselined  (exit 0)
#   FAIL  — <k> new engine-claiming spec(s) with no engine probe        (exit 1)
#   ERROR — nothing was checked                                        (exit 2)
#
# ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT=<path> additionally writes the sorted list of NEW
# (unbaselined) offenders to <path> on every verdict-producing run, including a
# PASS (an empty file). The delta helper reads it; its absence after an exit
# 0/1 run means the scan never reached comparison and is an ERROR to the caller.

set -u

GREP=/usr/bin/grep
[ -x "$GREP" ] || GREP=grep

ROOT="."
BASELINE=""
MODE=scan
LIST=0
REF=""
ROOT_GIVEN=0
BASELINE_GIVEN=0

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --selftest) MODE=selftest ;;
        --update-baseline) MODE=update ;;
        --list) LIST=1 ;;
        --root) shift; ROOT="${1:-.}"; ROOT_GIVEN=1 ;;
        --ref) shift; REF="${1:-}" ;;
        --baseline) shift; BASELINE="${1:-}"; BASELINE_GIVEN=1 ;;
        *) echo "unknown argument: $1" >&2; echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
done

# Fail closed on ambiguous input rather than silently picking a source of truth.
if [ -n "$REF" ]; then
    [ "$ROOT_GIVEN" -eq 0 ] || { echo "--ref and --root are mutually exclusive" >&2; echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
    [ "$MODE" != update ] || { echo "--update-baseline is not supported with --ref (it writes the working tree)" >&2; echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
    # In --ref mode the baseline is read FROM THE REF; an override would compare
    # committed content against a working-copy file that concurrent sessions
    # mutate, reintroducing exactly the hazard --ref exists to remove.
    [ "$BASELINE_GIVEN" -eq 0 ] || { echo "--baseline override is not supported with --ref" >&2; echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
fi

BASELINE_REL="scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
[ -n "$BASELINE" ] || BASELINE="$ROOT/$BASELINE_REL"

# Title-anchored: only a describe/it TITLE counts, never a comment or an import
# path. Whole-file matching flagged 677 files (mostly incidental mentions of the
# word "codegen"); title matching flags 228 real claims.
CLAIM_RE='^[[:space:]]*(describe|it|context|scenario)[[:space:]]+"[^"]*(jit|JIT|[Cc]ranelift|native codegen|natively|native lane|AOT|aot lane)'
PROBE_RE='engine_probe|run_under_engine|engine_stdout|engine_verdict_passes'
ACK_RE='@engine-reach:[[:space:]]*interpreter-only'
# (c) compiled-lane-by-real-compile marker + its plausibility evidence. Both
# halves must be present: something that actually asks for a native compile, and
# something that actually runs a process (to execute the produced binary).
NATIVE_ACK_RE='@engine-reach:[[:space:]]*native-by-compile'
NATIVE_COMPILE_RE='compile[^"]*--native|--native[^"]*-o|native-build|--mode=native'
NATIVE_EXEC_RE='rt_process_run|process_run|process_run_timeout|run_command|shell_exec|shell\('

# English words that merely CONTAIN a claim substring but claim nothing. `jit` is
# matched as a bare substring on purpose — the genuine claims are compound
# identifiers (`vulkan_jit`, `cuda_jit`, `jit_run`, `on_jit_compile`,
# `JitBackend`, ...), so a `\<jit\>` word-boundary tightening would over-exempt
# ~30 real claims. The only observed collision across the whole tree is the
# jitter family (7 titles: retry_resilience_spec x2, cron_jitter_config_spec,
# netcode_prediction_spec, simpleos_crypto_random_gate_spec x3). POSIX ERE has no
# lookaround, so instead of complicating CLAIM_RE we DELETE the decoy words from
# the text before matching. Verified 2026-08-09 that no other claim token
# (AOT / cranelift / natively / native codegen / native lane) has any such
# collision in spec titles.
DECOY_SED='s/[Jj][Ii][Tt][Tt][Ee][Rr]//g'

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the analyzer ---
# Every predicate below takes a BODY FILE — a local file whose contents are the
# spec text — never a repo-relative path. In --root mode the body file IS the
# working-tree file; in --ref mode it is a temp file holding
# `git cat-file blob <rev>:<path>`. That is the whole reason the two modes cannot
# drift: they differ only in how a body reaches this code, not in what it decides.
#
# is_engine_claiming BODYFILE -> 0 when the file needs a probe or an ack
is_engine_claiming() {
    sed "$DECOY_SED" "$1" 2>/dev/null | $GREP -qE "$CLAIM_RE"
}

is_exempt() {
    $GREP -qE "$PROBE_RE" "$1" 2>/dev/null && return 0
    $GREP -qE "$ACK_RE" "$1" 2>/dev/null && return 0
    has_native_by_compile "$1" && return 0
    return 1
}

# has_native_by_compile BODYFILE -> 0 when the file carries the native-by-compile
# marker AND its body backs the claim with a real compile+execute pattern.
# A marker with no evidence is NOT an escape hatch: it stays an offender.
has_native_by_compile() {
    $GREP -qE "$NATIVE_ACK_RE" "$1" 2>/dev/null || return 1
    $GREP -qE "$NATIVE_COMPILE_RE" "$1" 2>/dev/null || return 1
    $GREP -qE "$NATIVE_EXEC_RE" "$1" 2>/dev/null || return 1
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- selftest ---
# Every fixture asserts a DIRECTION the guard must get right. A guard that has
# never been observed failing is not a guard.
selftest() {
    tmp=$(mktemp -d) || { echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
    rc=0
    # The --ref fixtures re-invoke this script from INSIDE a throwaway git repo,
    # so $0 (typically a relative path) would not resolve there. Absolutise once.
    case "$0" in
        /*) SELF_ABS="$0" ;;
        *)  SELF_ABS="$(pwd)/$0" ;;
    esac
    fail() { echo "  selftest FAIL: $1"; rc=1; }

    # 1. a title claiming JIT is detected
    printf 'describe "codegen":\n    it "matches under the JIT":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/a_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/a_spec.spl" || fail "did not detect a JIT claim in an it title"

    # 2. a mere COMMENT mentioning the JIT is NOT a claim (no false positives on
    #    the 449 files that only discuss codegen in prose)
    printf '# this test is about the jit lowering\ndescribe "text":\n    it "sorts":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/b_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/b_spec.spl" && fail "flagged a comment-only mention as a claim"

    # 3. an import path mentioning codegen is NOT a claim
    printf 'use std.compiler.codegen.jit.{lower}\ndescribe "x":\n    it "works":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/c_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/c_spec.spl" && fail "flagged an import path as a claim"

    # 4. a claiming spec that uses the probe API is exempt
    printf 'use std.spec.engine_probe.{engine_stdout}\ndescribe "g":\n    it "agrees under the JIT":\n        expect(engine_stdout("p.spl", "jit")).to_contain("PASS")\n' > "$tmp/d_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/d_spec.spl" || fail "missed a claim in a probe-using spec"
    is_exempt "$tmp/d_spec.spl" || fail "probe-using spec was not exempt"

    # 5. the acknowledgement marker exempts
    printf '# @engine-reach: interpreter-only — asserts the lowering TABLE, not its execution\ndescribe "g":\n    it "lowers natively":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/e_spec.spl"
    is_exempt "$tmp/e_spec.spl" || fail "ack marker did not exempt"

    # 6. a claiming spec with neither is NOT exempt (the case that must FAIL)
    printf 'describe "g":\n    it "runs natively":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/f_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/f_spec.spl" || fail "missed a 'natively' claim"
    is_exempt "$tmp/f_spec.spl" && fail "unprobed unacked spec was treated as exempt"

    # 7. multiline: a claim on line 40 must be found, not just line 1 (the
    #    documented 'anchored regex without multiline' fail-open mode)
    { i=0; while [ $i -lt 40 ]; do echo "# filler"; i=$((i+1)); done
      printf 'describe "g":\n    it "is correct under cranelift":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n'; } > "$tmp/g_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/g_spec.spl" || fail "missed a claim below line 1"

    # 8. end-to-end: an unbaselined offender must make a full scan FAIL
    mkdir -p "$tmp/repo/test/x" "$tmp/repo/scripts/check"
    cp "$tmp/f_spec.spl" "$tmp/repo/test/x/new_spec.spl"
    : > "$tmp/repo/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    if ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 sh "$0" --root "$tmp/repo" >"$tmp/out" 2>&1; then
        fail "end-to-end scan passed with an unbaselined offender present"
    fi
    tail -n 1 "$tmp/out" | $GREP -q '^FAIL' || fail "end-to-end offender did not produce a FAIL verdict"

    # 9. end-to-end: baselining that same file makes it PASS
    echo "test/x/new_spec.spl" > "$tmp/repo/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 sh "$0" --root "$tmp/repo" >"$tmp/out2" 2>&1 || fail "baselined offender still failed"

    # 11. native-by-compile: marker BACKED by a real compile+execute exempts
    printf '# @engine-reach: native-by-compile — shells out to `compile --native` and runs the ELF\ndescribe "g":\n    it "is green under native codegen":\n        val (o, c) = shell(SIMPLE_BIN + " compile p.spl --native -o /tmp/p")\n        val (o2, c2) = shell("/tmp/p")\n        expect(o2).to_contain("ALL_OK")\n' > "$tmp/h_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/h_spec.spl" || fail "missed a 'native codegen' claim in a native-by-compile spec"
    is_exempt "$tmp/h_spec.spl" || fail "backed native-by-compile marker did not exempt"

    # 12. native-by-compile: the marker ALONE, with no compile+execute evidence,
    #     must NOT exempt — otherwise the new shape is an unverified escape hatch
    printf '# @engine-reach: native-by-compile — trust me\ndescribe "g":\n    it "is green under native codegen":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/i_spec.spl"
    is_exempt "$tmp/i_spec.spl" && fail "bare native-by-compile marker exempted with no compile+execute evidence"

    # 13. half the evidence is not evidence: a compile call with no execution
    printf '# @engine-reach: native-by-compile — half\ndescribe "g":\n    it "is green under native codegen":\n        expect(src).to_contain("native-build")\n' > "$tmp/j_spec.spl"
    is_exempt "$tmp/j_spec.spl" && fail "native-by-compile exempted on a compile mention with no execution"

    # 14. end-to-end: an unbaselined native-by-compile offender with a BARE
    #     marker must still make a full scan FAIL
    cp "$tmp/i_spec.spl" "$tmp/repo/test/x/bare_marker_spec.spl"
    : > "$tmp/repo/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    if ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 sh "$0" --root "$tmp/repo" >"$tmp/out4" 2>&1; then
        fail "end-to-end scan passed with a bare native-by-compile marker"
    fi
    rm -f "$tmp/repo/test/x/bare_marker_spec.spl"

    # 15. end-to-end: the BACKED marker needs no baseline entry at all
    rm -f "$tmp/repo/test/x/new_spec.spl"
    cp "$tmp/h_spec.spl" "$tmp/repo/test/x/backed_spec.spl"
    : > "$tmp/repo/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 sh "$0" --root "$tmp/repo" >"$tmp/out5" 2>&1 || fail "backed native-by-compile spec failed an end-to-end scan"

    # 16. "jitter" is NOT a JIT claim (the decoy-word false positive)
    printf 'describe "retry":\n    it "applies jitter to the backoff window":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/k_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/k_spec.spl" && fail "flagged the English word 'jitter' as a JIT claim"
    printf 'describe "crypto":\n    it "seeds from CPU Jitter entropy":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/l_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/l_spec.spl" && fail "flagged capitalised 'Jitter' as a JIT claim"

    # 17. the jitter exclusion must NOT over-exempt compound jit identifiers
    # (mixed-case `Jit...` — e.g. `JitBackend` — is a PRE-EXISTING blind spot of
    # CLAIM_RE, which lists only `jit|JIT`; it is unrelated to this exclusion and
    # widening it would add unbaselined offenders, so it is left recorded here
    # rather than silently changed.)
    for t in vulkan_jit cuda_jit jit_run on_jit_compile __jit__; do
        printf 'describe "g":\n    it "keeps %s stable":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' "$t" > "$tmp/m_spec.spl"
        is_engine_claiming "$tmp/m_spec.spl" || fail "lost a genuine claim for compound identifier $t"
    done

    # 18. a title carrying BOTH a decoy and a real claim is still a claim
    printf 'describe "g":\n    it "measures jitter under the JIT":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$tmp/n_spec.spl"
    is_engine_claiming "$tmp/n_spec.spl" || fail "decoy stripping swallowed a real JIT claim on the same line"

    # 19. --ref mode: the INCIDENT this mode exists to fix. A COMMITTED offender
    #     must be flagged, and an equivalent UNTRACKED file present only on disk
    #     must NOT be — an untracked scratch file from one of the ~16 concurrent
    #     agent lanes sharing this working copy cannot be in any push, so it must
    #     never block one.
    gr="$tmp/gitrepo"
    mkdir -p "$gr/test/x" "$gr/scripts/check"
    ( cd "$gr" \
      && git init -q . \
      && git config user.email t@t && git config user.name t \
      && cp "$tmp/f_spec.spl" test/x/committed_spec.spl \
      && : > scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt \
      && git add -A && git commit -qm base ) >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || fail "could not build the --ref selftest git repo"
    # untracked twin, on disk only, never committed
    cp "$tmp/f_spec.spl" "$gr/test/x/untracked_spec.spl"
    ( cd "$gr" && ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT="$tmp/ref.offenders" \
        sh "$SELF_ABS" --ref HEAD >"$tmp/ref.out" 2>&1 )
    refrc=$?
    [ "$refrc" -eq 1 ] || fail "--ref mode did not FAIL (exit 1) on a committed offender (got $refrc)"
    $GREP -qxF "test/x/committed_spec.spl" "$tmp/ref.offenders" 2>/dev/null \
        || fail "--ref mode did not flag the COMMITTED offender"
    if $GREP -qxF "test/x/untracked_spec.spl" "$tmp/ref.offenders" 2>/dev/null; then
        fail "--ref mode flagged an UNTRACKED on-disk file (the incident being fixed)"
    fi
    # ...and the offender list is written on a PASS too (empty file, not absent).
    rm -f "$gr/test/x/untracked_spec.spl"
    echo "test/x/committed_spec.spl" > "$gr/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    ( cd "$gr" && git add -A && git commit -qm baselined ) >/dev/null 2>&1
    ( cd "$gr" && ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT="$tmp/ref2.offenders" \
        sh "$SELF_ABS" --ref HEAD >"$tmp/ref2.out" 2>&1 )
    refrc2=$?
    [ "$refrc2" -eq 0 ] || fail "--ref mode did not PASS with the offender baselined AT THE REF (got $refrc2)"
    [ -f "$tmp/ref2.offenders" ] || fail "--ref mode did not write the offender list on a PASS"
    [ ! -s "$tmp/ref2.offenders" ] || fail "--ref mode wrote a non-empty offender list on a PASS"

    # 20. ANTI-DRIFT: on an identical, fully-committed tree with nothing
    #     untracked, --ref and --root must reach the same verdict and the same
    #     offender list. If the two modes ever fork their rules, this fixture is
    #     what catches it.
    gr2="$tmp/gitrepo2"
    mkdir -p "$gr2/test/x" "$gr2/scripts/check"
    cp "$tmp/f_spec.spl" "$gr2/test/x/one_spec.spl"
    cp "$tmp/d_spec.spl" "$gr2/test/x/probed_spec.spl"
    cp "$tmp/k_spec.spl" "$gr2/test/x/decoy_spec.spl"
    cp "$tmp/h_spec.spl" "$gr2/test/x/backed_spec.spl"
    printf 'describe "g":\n    it "runs under cranelift":\n        expect(1).to_equal(1)\n' > "$gr2/test/x/two_spec.spl"
    echo "test/x/two_spec.spl" > "$gr2/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    ( cd "$gr2" && git init -q . && git config user.email t@t && git config user.name t \
      && git add -A && git commit -qm base ) >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || fail "could not build the anti-drift selftest git repo"
    ( cd "$gr2" && ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT="$tmp/drift.ref" \
        sh "$SELF_ABS" --ref HEAD >"$tmp/drift.ref.out" 2>&1 )
    drc_ref=$?
    ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT="$tmp/drift.root" \
        sh "$0" --root "$gr2" >"$tmp/drift.root.out" 2>&1
    drc_root=$?
    [ "$drc_ref" -eq "$drc_root" ] \
        || fail "--ref and --root disagreed on exit code for an identical committed tree ($drc_ref vs $drc_root)"
    cmp -s "$tmp/drift.ref" "$tmp/drift.root" \
        || fail "--ref and --root produced different offender lists for an identical committed tree"
    # non-vacuity: this fixture must actually have found something to compare
    [ -s "$tmp/drift.ref" ] || fail "anti-drift fixture compared two EMPTY offender lists (vacuous)"

    # 21. fail-closed: --ref pointing at a non-commit is an ERROR, not a PASS
    ( cd "$gr2" && ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 sh "$SELF_ABS" --ref no/such/rev >"$tmp/ref3.out" 2>&1 )
    [ $? -eq 2 ] || fail "--ref with an unresolvable rev did not ERROR (exit 2)"

    # 10. fail-closed: an empty tree is an ERROR, not a PASS
    mkdir -p "$tmp/empty/scripts/check"
    : > "$tmp/empty/scripts/check/engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt"
    ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED=1 sh "$0" --root "$tmp/empty" >"$tmp/out3" 2>&1
    [ $? -eq 2 ] || fail "empty tree did not ERROR (exit 2)"

    rm -rf "$tmp"
    return $rc
}

if [ "$MODE" = selftest ]; then
    if selftest; then
        echo "PASS — 21 selftest fixtures checked"
        exit 0
    fi
    echo "FAIL — selftest fixtures failed"
    exit 1
fi

# selftest gates every real scan, and is fatal. The two end-to-end fixtures
# re-invoke this script as a real scan, which would re-enter the selftest
# forever; ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED marks those child runs so they skip the
# gate. It is set ONLY by the selftest itself, never by a caller, so a top-level
# run can never skip its gate.
if [ "${ENGINE_PROBE_GUARD_NESTED:-0}" != 1 ] && ! selftest >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "selftest failed — refusing to report a scan verdict" >&2
    echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"
    exit 2
fi

scanned=0
claiming=0
baselined=0
newoffenders=""
alloffenders=""

# Read baseline into a lookup file (POSIX sh has no arrays/assoc).
bl=$(mktemp) || { echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
body=$(mktemp) || { echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
: > "$bl"
: > "$bl.files"

# scan_one REL BODYFILE — the ONE classifier both modes go through.
scan_one() {
    _rel="$1"
    _body="$2"
    is_engine_claiming "$_body" || return 0
    claiming=$((claiming + 1))
    is_exempt "$_body" && return 0
    alloffenders="$alloffenders$_rel
"
    if $GREP -qxF "$_rel" "$bl" 2>/dev/null; then
        baselined=$((baselined + 1))
        return 0
    fi
    newoffenders="$newoffenders$_rel
"
}

if [ -n "$REF" ]; then
    git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${REF}^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || { echo "--ref '$REF' does not resolve to a commit" >&2; rm -f "$bl" "$bl.files" "$bl.all" "$body"; echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
    # Baseline read from the REF, not the working copy (see --ref rationale).
    if git cat-file -e "$REF:$BASELINE_REL" 2>/dev/null; then
        git show "$REF:$BASELINE_REL" 2>/dev/null \
            | sed 's/[[:space:]]*#.*$//; /^[[:space:]]*$/d' > "$bl"
    fi
    # `scanned` is the honest denominator: every spec file committed at the ref.
    git ls-tree -r --name-only "$REF" 2>/dev/null \
        | $GREP -E '^test/.*_spec\.spl$' | sort > "$bl.all"
    # PERFORMANCE, not a rule change. One `git cat-file` fork per spec is ~20k
    # forks and does not finish in a sane time. `git grep` at the ref narrows to
    # CANDIDATES first — and that narrowing is a strict SUPERSET of the claiming
    # set, because the only difference between this regex and the analyzer's is
    # the decoy-word strip (DECOY_SED), which can only ever REMOVE a match. Every
    # candidate is then classified by the SAME shared analyzer as --root mode; if
    # the prefilter were ever narrowed below the analyzer, fixture 20 (--ref vs
    # --root on an identical committed tree) is what would catch it.
    git grep -l -E "$CLAIM_RE" "$REF" -- 'test/' 2>/dev/null \
        | sed "s|^$REF:||" | $GREP -E '^test/.*_spec\.spl$' | sort > "$bl.files"
else
    [ -d "$ROOT/test" ] || { echo "no test/ directory under $ROOT" >&2; rm -f "$bl" "$bl.files" "$bl.all" "$body"; echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"; exit 2; }
    [ -f "$BASELINE" ] && sed 's/[[:space:]]*#.*$//; /^[[:space:]]*$/d' "$BASELINE" > "$bl"
    find "$ROOT/test" -name '*_spec.spl' -type f 2>/dev/null | sort > "$bl.files"
fi

if [ -n "$REF" ]; then
    scanned=$(wc -l < "$bl.all" | tr -d ' ')
else
    scanned=$(wc -l < "$bl.files" | tr -d ' ')
fi

if [ "$scanned" -eq 0 ]; then
    rm -f "$bl" "$bl.files" "$bl.all" "$body"
    echo "ERROR — nothing was checked"
    exit 2
fi

while IFS= read -r f; do
    if [ -n "$REF" ]; then
        git cat-file blob "$REF:$f" > "$body" 2>/dev/null || continue
        scan_one "$f" "$body"
    else
        rel=$(printf '%s' "$f" | sed "s|^$ROOT/||")
        scan_one "$rel" "$f"
    fi
done < "$bl.files"

if [ "$MODE" = update ]; then
    { echo "# engine_claiming_specs_baseline.txt — PRE-EXISTING debt, not approval."
      echo "# Each entry is a spec whose describe/it titles claim JIT / native / codegen /"
      echo "# AOT behaviour while its body physically runs on the tree-walking interpreter,"
      echo "# so it stays green through a full reintroduction of the defect it names."
      echo "# Generated by: sh scripts/check/check-engine-claiming-specs-use-probe.shs --update-baseline"
      echo "# REMOVING a line is how debt is retired (convert it to an engine probe, rename"
      echo "# the titles, or add '# @engine-reach: interpreter-only — <reason>')."
      echo "# ADDING a line must be justified in the same commit — that review checkpoint is"
      echo "# the entire point of this guard. Do not run --update-baseline to silence a new"
      echo "# failure."
      printf '%s' "$alloffenders" | sort -u; } > "$BASELINE.new"
    mv "$BASELINE.new" "$BASELINE"
    n=$($GREP -cv '^#' "$BASELINE" | tr -d ' ')
    rm -f "$bl" "$bl.files" "$bl.all" "$body"
    echo "PASS — baseline rewritten with $n entries"
    exit 0
fi

[ "$LIST" -eq 1 ] && [ -n "$newoffenders" ] && printf '%s' "$newoffenders"

nnew=$(printf '%s' "$newoffenders" | $GREP -c . 2>/dev/null || true)
[ -n "$nnew" ] || nnew=0

# Machine-readable offender list for the range-scoped delta helper. Written on
# every verdict-producing run, PASS included (then empty), so a MISSING file
# unambiguously means "this run never reached comparison".
if [ -n "${ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s' "$newoffenders" | $GREP -v '^$' | sort > "$ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT" 2>/dev/null \
        || : > "$ENGINE_CLAIMING_SPECS_OUT"
fi

rm -f "$bl" "$bl.files" "$bl.all" "$body"

if [ "$nnew" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo ""
    echo "These specs have a describe/it TITLE claiming JIT / native / codegen / AOT"
    echo "behaviour, but a spec body physically executes on the tree-walking"
    echo "interpreter (see the header of this script for the 4 stacked layers)."
    echo "Such a spec stays GREEN through a full reintroduction of the defect."
    echo ""
    printf '%s' "$newoffenders" | sed 's/^/  /'
    echo ""
    echo "Fix by ONE of:"
    echo "  1. Use an out-of-process engine probe (the supported mechanism):"
    echo "       use std.spec.engine_probe.{engine_stdout}"
    echo "       expect(engine_stdout(\"<probe>.spl\", \"jit\")).to_contain(\"PROBE VERDICT: PASS\")"
    echo "     The probe program MUST have \`fn main\`, MUST print a self-scored"
    echo "     verdict LINE (score the line, not the exit code), and MUST also be"
    echo "     asserted under \"interpret\" so two engines agreeing is the evidence."
    echo "     Guide: doc/07_guide/infra/testing/spec_engine_reach.md"
    echo "  2. Rename the titles so they no longer claim a compiled lane."
    echo "  3. If the spec deliberately asserts a lowering TABLE or a data"
    echo "     structure rather than compiled EXECUTION, record that:"
    echo "       # @engine-reach: interpreter-only — <why this is sufficient>"
    echo "  4. If the spec ALREADY reaches a compiled lane by shelling out to a"
    echo "     real \`compile --native\` / \`native-build\` and running the produced"
    echo "     binary, record that instead:"
    echo "       # @engine-reach: native-by-compile — <the compile+run it performs>"
    echo "     The guard verifies the claim: the body must contain BOTH a native"
    echo "     compile invocation AND a process-execution call."
    echo ""
    echo "FAIL — $nnew new engine-claiming spec(s) with no engine probe ($scanned scanned, $claiming engine-claiming)"
    exit 1
fi

echo "PASS — $scanned spec files scanned, $claiming engine-claiming, $baselined baselined"
exit 0
